{%block content%}
<h3>I. Abstract</h3>
<blockquote>
    This ongoing research project explores the capturing and decoding of electromyographic (EMG) signals from the vocal cords during silent reading. The objective is to translate sub-vocalized neuro-muscular impulses into actionable digital text without audible speech.
</blockquote>

<h3>II. Architecture & Signal Flow</h3>
<p>To adhere to a minimalist engineering approach, the hardware and software pipelines are strictly decoupled. Below is the current system schematic:</p>

<pre style="background-color: #f4f4f4; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid #ccc; overflow-x: auto;">
  [Throat Sensors] 
         |  (Micro-volt impulses)
         v
  +-------------+
  |  Amplifier  | ---> (Analog-to-Digital Conversion)
  +-------------+
         |
         v
  [Raw EMG Data] ---> [Feature Extraction] ---> [Classification Model]
</pre>

<h3>III. Current Phase</h3>
<ul>
    <li><b>Phase I:</b> Hardware assembly and electrode placement optimization. <i>(Complete)</i></li>
    <li><b>Phase II:</b> Noise filtering and baseline signal acquisition. <i>(In Progress)</i></li>
    <li><b>Phase III:</b> Training the classification model. <i>(Pending)</i></li>
</ul>

